Nomura has lowered its sum-of-the-parts target price for Aditya Birla Real Estate to Rs 1,600 from Rs 1,880 but retained its Buy rating, expecting pre-sales to bottom out in FY27 and recover as the launch pipeline strengthens.
The stock has declined 35% year-to-date, compared with a 13% fall in the Nifty, amid a limited new-launch pipeline in FY27 following weaker-than-expected business development in FY26 and volatility linked to the Middle East conflict. Nomura nevertheless sees improving business-development momentum, citing around Rs 60 billion in closures across three projects during the first half of FY27.
The brokerage expects the company to remain on track to achieve its long-term pre-sales guidance of Rs 150 billion by FY29, compared with around Rs 80 billion in FY26. It has cut its FY27 pre-sales estimate to Rs 50 billion, down 38% year-on-year, as the Niyaara Tower C launch has been deferred to Q2FY28.
Pre-sales in Q2FY27 were Rs 3 billion, down 67% year-on-year, although broadly in line with Nomura's estimate of Rs 4 billion. Collections rose 48% year-on-year. Earnings were supported by revenue recognition on the completion of the Navya and Tisya projects and an Rs 8.6 billion gain from the sale of the pulp and paper business.
The Niyaara Tower C launch has been delayed as the company redesigns the tower to add incremental value of Rs 19 billion; management now values the tower at Rs 79 billion. Nomura estimates FY27 launches of Rs 47 billion, concentrated in the second half, and expects second-half pre-sales of Rs 44 billion, including Rs 24 billion from new launches and Rs 20 billion from existing inventory.
FY28 launches exceeding Rs 100 billion are expected to include Niyaara Tower C, Vashi redevelopment and Hinjewadi in Pune. The company completed around Rs 60 billion in gross development value through three business-development transactions in the first half: Khar redevelopment, Vashi redevelopment and Pune Hinjawadi. Net debt stood at just Rs 3 billion at the end of Q2FY27.
Nomura raised its weighted average cost of capital estimate to 13.9% from around 12%, reflecting increased volatility, and cut its target price accordingly. The stock trades at a 26% discount to net asset value, according to the report.
